course: dictionary
lesson: 14053
title: Markedly: big enough to notice
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Markedly is an adverb, a word that tells you how something happens. It means in a clear way that is easy to notice. «Results improved markedly after practice.» The results got better, and the change was big enough for anyone to see.
explain: Use the words around it as clues. «The two paintings were markedly different in colour.» The word different tells you there was a difference, and markedly tells you the difference was easy to spot. To check your meaning, swap in noticeably and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: You will often meet markedly in reports, science writing and formal writing. «Her handwriting became markedly neater.» Neater is the clue: the change is clear. Remember the phrases markedly better, markedly different and markedly improved.
fact: Markedly is an adverb, a word that tells how something happens.
fact: «Markedly» means in a way that is clear and easy to notice.
fact: «Markedly» is often used with words like better, different and improved.
fact: A good swap: replace «markedly» with «noticeably», and check the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Markedly shows a big change, not a tiny one.
exercise: «The new bike was markedly faster than the old one.» What does markedly mean here? | Not at all | Slowly and quietly | Only just, a little bit | *Clearly and easy to notice
exercise: Sam's hair looked markedly different after the haircut. Which phrase means the same as markedly different? | *Clearly different | Almost the same | Slightly tired | Very quiet
exercise: «The temperature rose markedly during the afternoon.» Which swap keeps the same meaning? | *The temperature rose noticeably | The temperature fell slowly | The temperature rose by a tiny bit | The temperature rose quietly
test: «Her reading improved markedly over the year.» What does markedly tell you about the improvement? | *It was big and easy to see | It happened in the dark | It was so small nobody noticed | It stopped halfway
test: «The two gardens looked markedly different.» Which word means the same as markedly here? | Gently | Quietly | *Noticeably | Slowly
exam: Which word could replace markedly in «The team played markedly better in the second half»? | Quietly | Hardly | Rarely | *Clearly
